{"kind":"cas-record-v1","metadata":{"entries":[{"origin_package":"github.com/codalotl/codalotl/internal/tools/coretools","target_package":"github.com/codalotl/codalotl/internal/applypatch","identifier":"AffectedPaths","question":"For authorization-boundary tests, what exact patch examples demonstrate (1) operation headers accepted with surrounding whitespace while preserving path semantics, (2) a filename with intentional leading whitespace, and (3) aliases that resolve to one unique path? Please clarify whether callers should authorize the returned slice in one variadic Authorizer call or call once per path.","answer":"The existing tests demonstrate these cases exactly.\n\n1. Surrounding whitespace on operation headers\n\n```text\n  \n  *** Begin Patch  \n  *** Add File: added.txt  \n+new\n  *** Delete File: deleted.txt  \n  *** Update File: updated.txt  \n@@\n-old\n+new\n  *** Update File: moved.txt  \n  *** Move to: destination.txt  \n@@\n-before\n+after\n  *** End Patch  \n```\n\n`AffectedPaths(root, patch)` returns, in order:\n\n```text\n\u003croot\u003e/added.txt\n\u003croot\u003e/deleted.txt\n\u003croot\u003e/updated.txt\n\u003croot\u003e/moved.txt\n\u003croot\u003e/destination.txt\n```\n\nThe parser trims surrounding whitespace from header lines. Thus the two leading and trailing spaces around each header are structural padding, not part of the paths. Update moves include both source and destination.\n\n2. Intentional leading whitespace in a filename\n\n```text\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File:  leading.txt\n+new\n*** End Patch\n```\n\nThere are two spaces after the colon: one belongs to the `*** Add File: ` prefix, and the second becomes part of the filename. The returned path is:\n\n```go\nfilepath.Join(root, \" leading.txt\")\n```\n\n`ApplyPatch` likewise reports the relative path as `\" leading.txt\"`. Intentional trailing filename whitespace is not preserved because the complete header line is trimmed first.\n\n3. Lexical aliases resolving to one path\n\nThe test constructs this patch:\n\n```go\nabsolute := filepath.Join(root, \"same.txt\")\npatch := fmt.Sprintf(`*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: same.txt\n+first\n*** Add File: ./same.txt\n+second\n*** Add File: %s\n+third\n*** End Patch\n`, filepath.ToSlash(absolute))\n```\n\nAll three spellings resolve to the same cleaned absolute path, so the result is:\n\n```go\n[]string{absolute}\n```\n\nThis deduplication is lexical through `filepath.Clean`, `filepath.Rel`, and `filepath.Join`; `AffectedPaths` does not document filesystem or symlink canonicalization.\n\nAuthorization calling convention\n\n`applypatch` neither defines nor calls an `Authorizer`, so it does not document whether that API is batch- or per-path. It only guarantees that `AffectedPaths` returns the complete, unique absolute-path set in first-seen order, or `nil` with an error if any target is invalid.\n\nIf your variadic `Authorizer` defines one call as authorizing all supplied paths, use one call:\n\n```go\npaths, err := applypatch.AffectedPaths(root, patch)\nif err != nil {\n\treturn err\n}\nif err := authorizer.Authorize(paths...); err != nil {\n\treturn err\n}\n```\n\nOnly loop once per path if the `Authorizer` contract explicitly requires that. In either case, every returned path—including both sides of a move—must be authorized before calling `ApplyPatch`, using the same root and unchanged patch."}]},"additional_info":{"unix_timestamp":1784212894,"paths":["internal/applypatch/SPEC.md","internal/applypatch/affected_paths_test.go","internal/applypatch/apply_patch.go","internal/applypatch/apply_patch_codex_diff_test.go","internal/applypatch/apply_patch_codex_test.go","internal/applypatch/apply_patch_test.go","internal/applypatch/is_invalid_patch_test.go","internal/applypatch/replace.go","internal/applypatch/replace_test.go"],"git_clean":false,"git_commit":"14eb3660e86df4a5981af8038f7326d4fe95bc4d","git_merge_base":"2c988ca80141528947aa96ebbe860ac1b190ad82"}}